Hi Greg, Sasha,

Thanks for queuing the October 21st series, and to Sasha for the two
catches in it.
All 26 patches are in 6.12.115 and 6.18.60.

Here is what has been merged in mod-orphan since then, for 6.12.y and 6.18.=
y.
None of these 41 patches has been sent to stable before.
They fix memory-safety, locking and reference counting bugs, a few
protocol bugs that make links or calls hang, and three drivers (mkiss,
6pack, bpqether).

Drivers:
1. mkiss: size the frame buffers for worst-case KISS escaping
2. mkiss: don't use a tty write error as a byte count
3. bpqether: leave the Ethernet device's queue and counters alone
4. bpqether: fix truncated /proc/net/bpqether on partial reads
10. mkiss: restart the transmit queue when the interface is opened
11. 6pack: restart the transmit queue when the interface is opened

AX.25:
8. ax25: don't create a second connection for a link on an incoming SABM
9. ax25: free a closed socket when its device goes down
12. ax25: don't build a frame header from an uninitialised digipeater list
13. ax25: don't take a reference on a gone device in ax25_accept()
14. ax25: don't copy an uninitialised address to recvmsg()'s caller
15. ax25: free the old digipeater path of a listed socket under ax25_list_l=
ock
16. ax25: put an incoming connection on the listener's queue once it is set=
 up
17. ax25: don't test the socket flags of a connection that has no socket
18. ax25: free the copy of an IP packet that ax25_send_frame() turned down
19. ax25: don't leave half a message on a connection when fragmenting fails
20. ax25: make 16 the smallest maximum_packet_length of a port
21. ax25: keep the window below 8 when a socket goes back to modulo 8
22. ax25: let a socket connect again after a failed or ended connection
23. ax25: don't let connect() find its own socket when calling the same sta=
tion

NET/ROM:
6. netrom: ignore a BPQ transport timeout below the sysctl minimum
24. netrom: don't convert the maximum number of tries to jiffies
25. netrom: don't leak frames in nr_route_frame()
26. netrom: use the more flag of each frame taken from the resequencing que=
ue
27. netrom: give a frame back intact when it cannot be delivered
28. netrom: don't give the same circuit ID to two sockets
29. netrom: return the peer node in the address given by recvmsg()
30. netrom: keep the selected route when a route of a node is removed
31. netrom: drop a frame received with a TTL of 0
32. netrom: check the window size given by the peer
33. netrom: don't send part of a message that cannot be fragmented whole
34. netrom: disconnect sockets under their lock when a device goes down
35. netrom: only update the BPQ extension flag on circuit set-up frames

ROSE:
5. rose: arm the neighbour timers with mod_timer()
7. rose: don't give the same LCI to two calls placed at the same time
36. rose: hold the listener while a Call Request is queued on it
37. rose: hold the socket a received frame is delivered to
38. rose: report a timed out call to connect()
39. rose: drop the neighbour reference once when a cleared socket is closed
40. rose: swap the AX.25 connection of a neighbour under a lock
41. rose: give the sysctl timer limits in milliseconds

The numbers are the positions in the mboxes, which follow the
mod-orphan merge order.
That order must be kept, since several patches touch the same functions.

The two attached mboxes hold the 41 patches, one mbox per branch.
Each patch carries a "(cherry picked from commit <id>)" trailer with
the mod-orphan git id.

- 6.18.y: the mbox applies with "git am" on top of v6.18.62.
- 6.12.y: the mbox applies with "git am" on top of v6.12.116.

The backports are the mod-orphan commits with one change: in patch 5,
6.12 still has del_timer() instead of timer_delete(), so the context
of two functions is adjusted; a note in the patch says so.
Patches 1 to 4 had a "Co-Authored-By:" line for the AI assistant I
used; in the backports it is an "Assisted-by:" line, as
Documentation/process asks.
Patches 3 and 4 also get the Fixes tags that were missing in mod-orphan.

Testing:
- each patch builds on both branches (net/ax25, net/netrom, net/rose
and drivers/net/hamradio, W=3D1, KASAN enabled, mkiss, 6pack and
bpqether as modules), with no warnings and no errors;
- checkpatch reports nothing beyond style remarks inherited from the
original code;
- each fix was tested on a QEMU bench (KASAN and lockdep), as
described in its commit message;
- the AX.25, NET/ROM and ROSE fixes are part of the module set running
on two of our production nodes; patches 1 to 23 have been in service
since November 1st, 24 to 35 since November 4th, and 36 to 41 since
November 5th;
- no node uses bpqether or 6pack, so patches 3, 4 and 11 were only
tested on the bench.

Thanks,
Bernard


